Project Analysis: Binance Futures Order Bot
Objective
The project aimed to develop a CLI-based trading bot capable of placing various types of orders on the Binance USDT-M Futures Testnet, including market, limit, and advanced orders like OCO and TWAP. Emphasis was placed on robust input validation, clear logging, modular code design, and applicability in a testnet environment to facilitate safe experimentation.
Design & Architecture
Modular Structure: Order types were separated into distinct scripts (market_orders.py, limit_orders.py, oco.py, twap.py) for clarity, maintainability, and scalability.
API Integration: Leveraged the official python-binance client with configuration for connecting to the Binance Futures Testnet API.
Logging: A structured logging mechanism records every order attempt, API response, and errors into bot.log with timestamps, allowing effective auditing and debugging.
CLI Interface: User inputs such as symbol, side, quantity, order type, and prices were collected interactively, ensuring usability without command-line argument complexity.
Error Handling: Comprehensive exception handling ensures the bot gracefully manages invalid inputs and API issues, providing meaningful console feedback and logged diagnostics.
Implementation Highlights
Market & Limit Orders: Successfully implemented with checks for valid trading symbols, sides, quantities, and required prices for limit orders.
Advanced Orders: OCO order logic simulated by placing paired take-profit and stop-limit orders; TWAP strategy implemented by slicing large orders into smaller timed chunks.
Reproducibility: Scripted runs with configurable API keys and documented usage steps enable easy local execution and testing.
Compliance: The project meets submission requirements by maintaining a clean folder structure, detailed README, generated logs, and an accompanying report PDF.
Testing & Results
All core functionalities were tested against Binance Futures Testnet endpoints, confirming correct order placement and error reporting.
Log files captured detailed traces of operations, validating the robustness of API calls.
Interactive prompts ensured the program is user-friendly and adaptable to various trading scenarios.
Advanced orders demonstrated the potential for complex trade automation, setting the stage for future enhancements.
Challenges & Future Enhancements
Order Cancellation for OCO: Automated cancellation of one leg of OCO upon execution of the other was not implemented, highlighting an area for further development.
Enhanced Validation: Adding symbol and market state validation from Binance API to preempt invalid orders.
User Experience: Improving CLI with command history, input validation loops, and help commands for better usability.
Additional Strategies: Implementing grid trading, risk management, and real-time data integration like Fear & Greed Index to enhance bot intelligence.







